\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::1>
\f2\b\fs56 How personality-driven livestreamers become successful: A case-study guide\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::1>
\f0\fs28 The most interesting thing about successful livestreamers is that 
\f3\b many of them aren't primarily successful because of what they do on camera. They're successful because of the experience they create around doing it.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Someone can become enormously popular by walking through a city, repairing electronics, baking, talking about the news, listening to music, or simply sitting at a desk. What makes the difference is the combination of personality, audience participation, distinctive circumstances, and the reasons people develop a habit of returning.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Your areas of interest\'97Just Chatting, lifestyle, IRL, workbench, and reactions\'97are especially rich because they offer several fundamentally different paths to success.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I'll distinguish between 
\f3\b superstar outliers, successful specialist creators, and unusual formats
\f0\b0 . That's important because what works for someone with millions of followers is not necessarily what works for someone trying to build a sustainable audience of a few hundred or a few thousand regular viewers.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

