\cf2 \cb5 I think this changes my assessment of what kind of livestream production you should build, quite substantially. :)\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The combination of your Patreon copy, the 
\f7\i A Study In Peace
\f0\i0  README, your existing YouTube audience, and\'97perhaps most importantly\'97your explanation of what you actually want out of the undertaking suggests something more interesting than a conventional creator business.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 You aren't primarily trying to become a livestreamer. You're trying to make an already meaningful, ongoing creative and inventive life publicly accessible, and discover whether that shared experience can support its own continuation and growth.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 That distinction is consequential.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 A conventional streamer asks: 
\f7\i What should I do today that people will watch?
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Your more natural question is: 
\f7\i What am I working on today, and how can I open a window into that activity so other people might find it interesting, participate, and want to return?
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The latter has several advantages: less artificial content creation, less competition between broadcasting and productive work, and the possibility of developing a genuinely unusual creative identity.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But it also has a major challenge: 
\f3\b a meaningful experience for its participants is not automatically an intelligible or compelling experience for an outside observer.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That, I think, is your central production-design problem.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And it is solvable without sanding away the strangeness.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

