\f3\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 People who want to understand
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 They ask questions, discuss the architecture, investigate the source materials, challenge propositions, and follow your experiments.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f12\fs40 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs24 \cf2 \cb12 Invent\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 People who want to contribute
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 They propose tests, report findings, fork code and texts, make independent inventions, offer resources or collaborate on well-defined projects.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 These are participation levels, not financial membership tiers or ranks of importance.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I find this particularly compatible with your World Piece Computer approach: people can participate locally, through their own agency, without surrendering their intellectual independence to the larger program.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It's also compatible with a charitable or community-oriented undertaking. A person who cannot contribute financially might nevertheless make a valuable intellectual, artistic, or practical contribution.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

