\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 So perhaps the optimal first impression isn't "I promise this is a perfectly ordinary scientific endeavor."
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Perhaps it's more like: 
\f7\i "Welcome. This is obviously ridiculous. We're doing it anyway. Would you like to see what happens?"
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The remarkable advantage of that approach is that it doesn't require a newcomer to understand General Peace Dynamics, the World Piece Computer, the human\'96AI relationship, or any of the underlying philosophical machinery before enjoying the performance.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The absurdity can carry the invitation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 There is, however, one distinction I would preserve:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 Make the premise absurd. Make the immediate activity legible.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
