\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 The first two paragraphs aren't a conventional description. They're a tiny continuation of your 2022 metafiction.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That strikes me as potentially powerful. There's even a little historical continuity built into it: the manuscript describes a character trying to make a strange fictional world communicable, and four years later you're experimenting with a different medium for opening that world to others.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The line 
\f3\b "Now the book has escaped into a livestream"
\f0\b0  is doing several jobs simultaneously. It's an invitation, a joke, a narrative bridge, and a reasonable description of the move from manuscript to public production.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Then the copy switches registers.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The funding information is straightforward. The introduction tells people who you are. The fuller description makes the range of activities understandable. And the closing returns to the program's native language.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I'm particularly fond of keeping your mission statement essentially untouched at the bottom.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You needn't make every sentence equally comprehensible to every person. In fact, the unfamiliar vocabulary can be part of the experience of discovering the project.\cf0 \cb1 \
