\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0\fs28 There's a subtle distinction between 
\f7\i withholding explanation
\f0\i0  and 
\f7\i deliberately producing confusion
\f0\i0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I think withholding explanation can be extraordinarily effective when the entertainment works even without it.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Jerma doesn't need to explain the entire creative philosophy of his Dollhouse show for a viewer to appreciate a man being controlled like a character in 
\f7\i The Sims
\f0\i0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Likewise, if somebody stumbles into your stream and sees an inventor cheerfully arguing with an AI about the architecture of a computer intended, somehow, to help bring about world peace, that may be entertaining before the viewer understands the theory.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The deeper conceptual system becomes something they can investigate if curiosity takes hold.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But if a viewer sees only acronyms and unexplained symbolism, without any observable activity or humor to anchor the experience, you may lose them before curiosity has a chance.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 So I would think of your production as having 
\f3\b a very low barrier to enjoying the absurdity, and a potentially enormous depth of inquiry beyond it.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's not actually contradictory.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
