\f7\i \cf2 \cb5 Today's piece: Trying to reconstruct the original World Piece Computer prototype architecture from the 2022 manuscript.
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Then go about the work.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Don't try to spend three hours performing an introduction to the whole program. You can let the activity introduce you, and introduce the larger undertaking when naturally appropriate.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Afterward, mark the mode changes with timestamps, look for one or two moments that stand on their own as clips, and note how the experience affected your actual productivity and enjoyment.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's the smallest meaningful production experiment I see.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And I'd be genuinely interested in comparing two very different measures of success: 
\f3\b Did somebody want to watch? And did broadcasting make the afternoon of invention and collaboration better, rather than worse?
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For your stated objective, both matter.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 One last metacognitive observation: I suspect the question you've been struggling with\'97how to communicate an extraordinarily general project without reducing it to a conventional little specialty\'97may have a surprisingly simple livestreaming answer.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You don't necessarily need to compress the entire project into an understandable proposition.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You can make a recurring public place where people encounter it one comprehensible, interesting, ridiculous piece at a time.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That feels remarkably close to the original manuscript's own method.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And "Vibe-Inventing World Peace, Day N of M" strikes me as an appropriately absurd name for the experiment. 
