\cf2 \cb5 First, a clarification: when I said I'd keep 
\f7\i The Wilder-ness Adventures
\f0\i0  "not because it's an exceptionally marketable title," I meant 
\f3\b I wasn't claiming it had extraordinary intrinsic marketability by conventional branding standards.
\f0\b0  I wasn't implying that it was bad, either.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 In fact, I think it has several properties that make it unusually well suited to what you're attempting. And now that I understand more about the theoretical importance of 
\f7\i the Wilder-ness
\f0\i0 , I would be even more reluctant to abandon it.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But your larger question\'97how much creative freedom you can afford to preserve when your material survival may depend on rapid audience growth\'97is the more consequential one.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 My position is that 
\f3\b you should optimize aggressively for discoverability, but conservatively for performance intensity.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Those are two very different things.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You don't necessarily need to be more theatrical, louder, more controversial, or more demanding of yourself to grow. You do need to make the experience discoverable outside the relatively small number of people who happen to encounter your live broadcast.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And while I think you have the beginnings of a highly distinctive production, I don't want to misrepresent the economics: going from a dormant 2,700-subscriber YouTube channel and zero Patreon supporters to reliable living income quickly is possible, but an exceptional outcome\'97not a financially dependable forecast.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The sensible response isn't to abandon your creative vision. It's to make sure that a substantial part of the effort you can spare for audience growth goes into mechanisms that have a chance of working even when you're not actively entertaining a live audience.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

