\f0\fs28 I see four distinct advantages.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 First, it's a play on your name. Second, it evokes exploration, unpredictability, and the unknown. Third, the definite article gives it a slightly theatrical, literary character. Fourth\'97and probably most importantly\'97it already belongs to the intellectual world you're constructing.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The problem you identified is real: 
\f3\b spoken aloud, "The Wilder-ness Adventures" is nearly indistinguishable from "the wilderness adventures."
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 When written, the hyphen carries substantial semantic weight, but only for somebody who notices it.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And in search results, thumbnails, mobile interfaces, and conversational recommendations, that distinction can be lost.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I would distinguish between the enduring brand and the individual broadcast title.\cf0 \cb1 \
