\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::3>
\f8\b\fs40 How many clips should you produce?\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0\fs28 For the initial phase, my suggested target is 
\f3\b one worthwhile published clip per stream, with two or three candidates marked during the broadcast
\f0\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Not ten Shorts a day. Not obsessive self-surveillance.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 If a broadcast genuinely produces three good clips, wonderful. If it produces none, don't force one into existence.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 However, I would also deliberately test short explanatory videos created 
\f7\i outside
\f0\i0  the livestream. Sometimes an excellent three-hour exploration produces a finding that's best communicated afterward in a fresh 90-second explanation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That may actually be much more efficient than extracting an awkward excerpt.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The objective is not simply to publish livestream fragments.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It is to generate independently useful material that attracts people interested in your particular world.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f4\b\fs48 3. How much should you engage with an audience?\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 At the beginning, audience interaction serves a different function from mass-audience entertainment.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 With zero or two people watching, you don't have enough social activity to sustain an open-ended chat program. If you wait for questions and reactions, you may feel like you're performing to an empty room.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 So I would build a show that remains satisfying when nobody is watching.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb5 Treat chat as an opportunity to enrich the work, not as the engine required to make the work happen.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Early engagement is still extremely valuable for retention. A newcomer who asks an intelligent question and receives a thoughtful answer has a reason to return. A person who makes a useful suggestion and later sees it incorporated has a tangible connection to the undertaking.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But you don't need to make yourself perpetually available.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I'd experiment with periodic chat check-ins rather than nonstop attention. You might work for twenty minutes, glance over, answer an interesting question, then return to the problem.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 For cognitively demanding tasks, even that may be too frequent. You can explicitly say you're entering a concentration period.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And if someone proposes something useful, note it. They may be helping create your future program, not merely filling airtime.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Over time, as a community develops, participation itself can become a growth mechanism.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Initially, though, 
\f3\b the person who has to show up for every episode is you. The audience doesn't have to arrive for the activity to be worthwhile.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

