\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Waiting for advertising revenue would therefore be an especially uncertain immediate income strategy.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I would prioritize 
\f3\b direct voluntary support alongside audience discovery
\f0\b0 , rather than treating support as something to introduce after you become popular.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That means a brief public launch announcement, a clear Patreon goal, a one-time support option, and periodic factual progress updates. Your existing subscribers should have an opportunity to discover that the project has returned before you're relying entirely on algorithmic recommendations.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 A practical funding update might explain what a particular month's contributions would sustain, what you actually accomplished, and what resources are still missing.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Because this is currently a livelihood problem as well as an invention program, I'd be explicit about that. People can decide whether they want to support your continued ability to work.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 I wouldn't make success sound inevitable or imply a donation is guaranteed to produce a scientific breakthrough.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And I wouldn't wait for a viral moment to make the appeal.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 If your housing is at risk on a short timeline, I would not treat an unproven creator-growth strategy as a reliable substitute for an immediate housing contingency. The project may gain traction, but no ethical analysis of livestream economics can promise that in time.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::3>
\f8\b\fs40 Do you need to become more outrageous?\cf0 \cb1 \
